{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:inside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:2ea06457020f8596c9d30181a59d98fcb6ff9bbb87d4dbeebfc24ea8150655ce","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.1},{"probability":0.5,"value":2},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.1}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Starting point reconstructed from 2A plus G7 GDP weights (US ~45%, Japan ~10%, Germany ~10%, UK ~7%, France ~7%, Italy ~5%, Canada ~5%): 0.45(2.60) + 0.10(0.6) + 0.10(0.9) + 0.07(1.6) + 0.07(1.2) + 0.05(1.9) + 0.05(1.7) = ~1.70% today. Mechanism to 2032-36, decomposed into r* and term premium. r* DOWN: the 50-64 pre-retirement bulge keeps desired saving high through the early 2030s (the dissaving cohort is not yet dominant in the OECD, unlike Japan/Italy); g falls to my CQ3 1.2% OECD figure, and capital-concentrated income per BIS Box C has a higher saving propensity, so the demand-shortfall channel is worth -40 to -70bp. TERM PREMIUM UP: gross public debt >100% of GDP by 2029 with net interest 2->3% of GDP and P95 Debt-at-Risk 117%, care and pension claims that cannot be deferred because the care shadow price is rising (my weakest link is itself a fiscal object), eroding sovereign convenience yields, and basis-trade/hedge-fund absorption replacing price-insensitive official and bank demand - worth +70 to +110bp. IMF's (r-g) worsening by +1.0pp is the net of these in the same direction I derive. Net P50 = 1.70 - 0.55 + 0.90 = ~2.05 -> 2.0%. P10 = 1.1%: post-impairment demand shortfall plus financial repression / renewed central-bank duration absorption (the D regime, where the care bottleneck is suppressed by rationing rather than spending). P90 = 3.1%: fiscal dominance with the care/pension claim monetized at the long end and the term premium repricing, which is the same world as CQ6-C. Japan's weight is the main anchor keeping the P50 near 2 rather than above it, and that anchor is itself eroding.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.122Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US NCLEX-RN first-time candidate volume plus OECD-wide nursing-programme completions: do annual completions in the US, Germany, Japan, Korea and the UK rise by more than 25% above their 2025 level, with the binding constraint (clinical placement slots and nursing faculty posts) publicly reported as eased rather than worsened? Publicly observable via NCSBN annual NCLEX statistics, OECD Health Statistics 'nursing graduates', and national regulator registers.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the single cleanest falsifier of my weakest-link claim. If completions are up >25% AND placement/faculty capacity is reported as eased, my e_supply of 0.60 and my faculty/preceptor bottleneck are both wrong. CQ3 P50 moves up ~+0.25pp to ~2.05%, CQ6-A rises from 12 to ~20 and C falls from 23 to ~17, CQ1's unemployment conjunct strengthens while its wage conjunct weakens (more supply, less wage pressure), leaving CQ1 roughly flat near 16, and CQ2's OECD leg drops ~4pp because the Baumol pass-through shrinks.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 care-sector worker admissions by credential-recognition route: do combined annual entries of foreign-trained nurses, aides and personal-care workers exceed 1.2M/yr in any single year, and does mean time-to-licensure for an India- or Philippines-trained nurse fall below 12 months in at least three of the US, Germany, Japan, Canada and the UK? Observable via OECD International Migration Outlook, national regulator throughput statistics, and Philippine POEA/DMW deployment data.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\